5:[["$","$L10","5.12",{"topic":{"id":"5.12","title":"Future Trends in Cyber Forensics, AI and Cyber Security","minutes":13,"summary":"Prepare for changing evidence sources, stronger provenance, privacy-preserving methods, and accountable automation.","sections":[{"title":"New evidence environments","body":["Evidence will increasingly span cloud services, connected vehicles, wearables, industrial systems, and AI-mediated interactions. Each source has its own data model, retention, ownership, and access constraints. The forensic challenge is not only volume; it is understanding which system generated a record and what transformations occurred before collection.","Synthetic-media detection and provenance will remain complementary. A detector can produce false positives and may degrade as generation methods change. Provenance mechanisms can help establish a recorded history, but absence of provenance does not prove fabrication. Legal and technical analysis must keep pace with the actual notified rules and available standards."]},{"title":"Automation with accountable boundaries","body":["AI-assisted triage, cross-source correlation, and automated reporting can reduce repetitive work. They also introduce risks of hidden bias, data leakage, invented details, and overreliance. Design systems with explicit authority boundaries, audit logs, human review, and a way to reconstruct how an output was produced.","Privacy-preserving collaboration, improved cloud auditability, and standardized exchange formats may support investigations without unnecessary raw-data sharing. These are developing capabilities, not universal solutions. Evaluate claims against a defined use case, independent testing, and the applicable legal framework rather than assuming a technology removes governance problems."]},{"title":"A professional learning plan","body":["Build competence in legal interpretation, evidence handling, operating systems, networks, and critical evaluation of models. Practise explaining uncertainty to non-specialists. Follow official amendments, current case law, supported tool documentation, and peer-reviewed research. A tool certificate alone does not establish broad forensic competence.","Use the course’s case files and simulations to connect the units: identify the legal issue, preserve the source, test alternative explanations, evaluate the evidence, and communicate the result. The durable skill is a disciplined method. Technologies will change, but authority, integrity, reproducibility, and fairness remain central."]}],"practice":{"title":"Design your next investigation","scenario":"Choose one fictional case from the reading room and imagine a new cloud or AI evidence source.","steps":["Write the legal and technical questions.","Identify the source, controller, retention, and lawful access route.","Plan verification, human review, and alternative explanations.","Prepare a printable evidence plan with sources and limitations."],"debrief":"A future-ready investigator combines curiosity with boundaries: test new methods, preserve the evidence, and remain accountable for the conclusion."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["bsa","it-2021","dpdp"],"sources":["it2026","nist-csf","owasp-ml","enisa"],"labId":"ai","unit":5,"objectives":["Explain new evidence environments and apply it to an investigation.","Explain automation with accountable boundaries and apply it to an investigation.","Explain a professional learning plan and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-5.12","prompt":"Which capability is most durable as forensic technology changes?","options":["Memorizing one tool’s menu.","A disciplined method of authority, preservation, validation, and clear reasoning.","Trusting every automated score.","Assuming provenance is never relevant."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Tools change; accountable investigative reasoning remains the foundation of reliable forensic work.","unit":5}}}]]
